  VALUES (v_test, $q$reading1$q$, $q$Reading — Passage 1$q$, $q$reading$q$, 20, NULL, $q$Urban Beekeeping

Over the past two decades, beekeeping has quietly moved from the countryside to the city. Rooftop hives now sit above offices in London, Paris and New York, and municipal governments in several countries encourage residents to keep bees on balconies and in community gardens. Supporters argue that cities can actually be better habitats for bees than modern farmland. Intensive agriculture often relies on a single crop and on pesticides, leaving bees with little to eat for much of the year. By contrast, parks, gardens and street trees provide a wide range of flowers that bloom at different times from spring to autumn.

However, the trend has its critics. Some ecologists warn that a sharp increase in honeybee colonies may put pressure on wild pollinators such as bumblebees and solitary bees, which compete for the same nectar. A study in one European capital found that in areas with the most hives, wild bee visits to flowers were noticeably lower. The researchers did not claim that honeybees were the only cause, but they recommended that cities plant more flowers before allowing additional hives.

Honey produced in cities has also attracted attention. Tests show that it is generally safe to eat, since bees filter out most of the heavy metals found in urban air. Beekeepers say that city honey tastes different from country honey because the bees feed on dozens of garden plants rather than one crop.$q$, NULL, 0)
  RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Municipal governments in some countries encourage residents to keep bees.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The text says municipal governments in several countries encourage residents to keep bees.$q$, 0) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Farmland always offers bees more food than cities do.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The text argues that cities can be better habitats than modern farmland.$q$, 1) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$In areas with the most hives, wild bees visited flowers less often.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$A study found wild bee visits were noticeably lower where hives were most numerous.$q$, 2) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$The researchers proved that honeybees were the only cause of fewer wild bees.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$They 'did not claim that honeybees were the only cause'.$q$, 3) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$City honey is more expensive than country honey.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The passage does not mention the price of honey.$q$, 4) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What did the researchers recommend?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$They recommended that cities plant more flowers before allowing additional hives.$q$, 5) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$reading2$q$, $q$Reading — Passage 2$q$, $q$reading$q$, 20, NULL, $q$Sleep and Memory

Psychologists have long known that sleep helps people remember what they have learned, but only recently have they begun to understand how. During the deepest stages of sleep, the brain replays patterns of activity that occurred while we were awake, strengthening the connections that matter and allowing others to fade. This process, known as consolidation, appears to move new information from the hippocampus, where memories are first stored, to the cortex, where they can be kept for years.

In one well-known experiment, two groups of students learned a list of word pairs. One group learned the list in the evening and slept before being tested; the other learned it in the morning and stayed awake all day. When both groups were tested twelve hours later, the sleepers recalled around 20 per cent more pairs. The difference was largest for pairs that were hard to remember.

These findings have practical consequences. Many students believe that staying up all night to study is an efficient strategy, yet the evidence suggests the opposite. A short nap after learning can also help, but it cannot fully replace a night of normal sleep. Researchers add one note of caution: the studies so far involve mostly young, healthy adults, so it is not yet clear whether the same effects apply to older people.$q$, NULL, 1)
  RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$According to the passage, consolidation is the process by which…$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$Consolidation moves information from the hippocampus to the cortex, where it can be kept for years.$q$, 0) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$In the experiment, the students who slept…$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The sleepers recalled around 20 per cent more pairs.$q$, 1) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$The difference between the two groups was largest for…$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The difference was largest for pairs that were hard to remember.$q$, 2) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$The writer believes that staying up all night to study is an efficient strategy.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The writer says the evidence suggests the opposite.$q$, 3) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$A short nap can completely replace a night of normal sleep.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$A nap can help, but 'it cannot fully replace a night of normal sleep'.$q$, 4) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$The studies so far have involved people of a wide range of ages.$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The studies mostly involve young, healthy adults.$q$, 5) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$listening1$q$, $q$Listening — Section 1$q$, $q$listening$q$, 10, $q$لا يتضمن هذا القسم ملفًا صوتيًا بعد — اقرأ نص التسجيل كما لو كنت تسمعه (مرة واحدة فقط في الامتحان الحقيقي)، ثم أجب عن الأسئلة.$q$, $q$Telephone conversation: joining a sports centre

Receptionist: Good morning, Riverside Sports Centre. How can I help?
Customer: Hi, I'd like to join the centre. I'm a student at the university.
Receptionist: Certainly. We have a student membership at thirty-five pounds a month, which includes the gym and the swimming pool. Classes such as yoga are an extra eight pounds each.
Customer: Is there a joining fee?
Receptionist: Normally it's fifty pounds, but it's free this month for students.
Customer: Great. What are the opening hours?
Receptionist: Monday to Friday from six in the morning until ten at night, and on weekends from eight until six.
Customer: And do I need to bring anything to sign up?
Receptionist: Just your student card and a passport-size photo. You can complete the form online if you like.
Customer: I'll come in on Thursday afternoon, then.
Receptionist: Perfect. Ask for Mrs Patel; she'll show you round.$q$, NULL, 2)
  RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$How much is the monthly student membership?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Thirty-five pounds a month.'$q$, 0) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What is the joining fee for students this month?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'It's free this month for students.'$q$, 1) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What are the weekend opening hours?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'On weekends from eight until six.'$q$, 2) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What must the customer bring to sign up?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Just your student card and a passport-size photo.'$q$, 3) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$When will the customer visit the centre?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'I'll come in on Thursday afternoon.'$q$, 4) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$listening2$q$, $q$Listening — Section 2$q$, $q$listening$q$, 10, $q$لا يتضمن هذا القسم ملفًا صوتيًا بعد — اقرأ نص التسجيل كما لو كنت تسمعه (مرة واحدة فقط في الامتحان الحقيقي)، ثم أجب عن الأسئلة.$q$, $q$Talk: orientation tour of the university library

"Welcome to the university library. I'll give you a brief tour. We are on the ground floor, where you will find the help desk and the café. Please note that food and drink are allowed only here, not on the upper floors. The first floor contains the main book collection and the silent study area, where phones must be switched off. The second floor has group study rooms; these can be booked online for up to two hours at a time. The third floor holds the archives, and you need a special permission card to enter. The library closes at midnight during exam weeks, but at ten in the evening for the rest of the year. If you need to borrow a laptop, ask at the help desk; you can keep one for four hours."$q$, NULL, 3)
  RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Where is the café?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'On the ground floor, where you will find the help desk and the café.'$q$, 0) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Where must phones be switched off?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'The silent study area, where phones must be switched off.'$q$, 1) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$For how long can a group study room be booked?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Up to two hours at a time.'$q$, 2) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What do you need in order to enter the archives?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'You need a special permission card to enter.'$q$, 3) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What time does the library normally close?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$Midnight only in exam weeks; 'ten in the evening for the rest of the year'.$q$, 4) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$How long can a student keep a borrowed laptop?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'You can keep one for four hours.'$q$, 5) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$Writing — Task 2 (ungraded practice)$q$, 40, $q$اكتب مقالًا من 250 كلمة على الأقل. هذا تدريب ذاتي؛ قارن نصك بالنموذج بعد الانتهاء.$q$, $q$WRITING TASK 2 (about 40 minutes)

You should spend about 40 minutes on this task.

Write about the following topic:

Some people believe that university students should study only subjects that will help them find a job. Others think that students should be free to study any subject they enjoy.

Discuss both views and give your own opinion.

Give reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.

Write at least 250 words.$q$, $q$Education is often described as preparation for working life, yet it is also a chance to develop curiosity. Opinions differ on whether students should choose only job-oriented subjects or follow their interests, and I believe a balance between the two serves most students best.

Those who support practical degrees make a reasonable point. University is expensive, and graduates who cannot find work may struggle to repay their loans. Subjects such as engineering, medicine or computer science lead to clear careers, and governments need more people with these skills. For a student from a family with limited resources, choosing a course with good job prospects can feel like the only responsible decision.

On the other hand, students who study what they enjoy tend to work harder and achieve better results. A person who is fascinated by history or philosophy will read more widely and think more deeply than someone who is bored by a "useful" course. Moreover, the labour market changes quickly. Many jobs that exist today did not exist twenty years ago, so skills such as critical thinking, writing and problem solving may be more valuable than any single technical subject.

In my opinion, students should not be forced to choose one path. Universities could encourage them to combine interests with practical skills, for example by studying literature alongside data analysis, or art alongside business. This approach would keep students motivated while also improving their employability.

In conclusion, although job-focused degrees offer security, freedom of choice produces more engaged graduates, and a mixture of both is the most sensible solution.$q$);
END $ep$;

