  DELETE FROM public.test_writing_prompts WHERE test_id = v_test;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$reading1$q$, $q$Reading — Academic passage$q$, $q$reading$q$, 18, NULL, $q$The Origins of Writing

Writing is one of humanity's most important inventions, yet it appeared surprisingly late in history. For tens of thousands of years, people communicated only through speech, images and objects. The earliest known writing system emerged in Mesopotamia around 3400 BCE, when merchants in the city of Uruk began pressing marks into soft clay tablets. These first marks were not designed to record stories or poems; they were a practical tool for keeping track of goods such as grain, sheep and beer.

Scholars believe the system developed from small clay tokens that had been used for centuries as counters. A token in the shape of a cone might represent a measure of grain, for instance. When traders began sealing tokens inside clay envelopes, they also pressed the tokens' shapes onto the outside so that the contents could be checked without breaking the envelope. Eventually, people realised that the marks alone were enough, and the tokens were no longer necessary.

Over the following centuries the marks became more abstract. Scribes used a reed pen with a wedge-shaped tip, which gave the script its modern name, cuneiform, from the Latin word for "wedge". Gradually the signs came to represent the sounds of spoken words as well as objects, allowing scribes to write names, letters and eventually literature. The same pattern appears to have occurred independently in Egypt, China and Central America, which suggests that writing arises naturally wherever societies become complex enough to need records.$q$, NULL, 0)
  RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$According to paragraph 1, the first marks on clay tablets were mainly used to…$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$They were 'a practical tool for keeping track of goods'.$q$, 0) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$The word "abstract" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to…$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'More abstract' means the marks looked less and less like the real objects they represented.$q$, 1) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Why did traders press the tokens' shapes onto the outside of the clay envelopes?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$So that contents could be checked without breaking the envelope.$q$, 2) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Which of the following is TRUE about cuneiform?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The name comes from the Latin word for 'wedge'.$q$, 3) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What can be inferred about writing from the final sentence of the passage?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The same pattern occurred independently, suggesting writing arises wherever societies become complex.$q$, 4) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What happened to the tokens, according to the passage?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'The tokens were no longer necessary.'$q$, 5) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$listening1$q$, $q$Listening — Lecture$q$, $q$listening$q$, 10, $q$لا يتضمن هذا القسم ملفًا صوتيًا بعد — اقرأ نص التسجيل كما لو كنت تسمعه (مرة واحدة فقط في الامتحان الحقيقي)، ثم أجب عن الأسئلة.$q$, $q$Lecture — Astronomy: Why Is Mars Red?

Professor: Last week we talked about the surface of Mars. Today I want to explain why the planet looks red from Earth. The answer is rust. The Martian soil contains a lot of iron, and over billions of years that iron has reacted with oxygen to form iron oxide, which is the same substance we see when a bicycle rusts.
Now, you might ask, where did the oxygen come from if there is hardly any in the atmosphere today? Scientists think that in the distant past Mars had liquid water, and water molecules can release oxygen when they are broken apart by sunlight. Some of that oxygen combined with the iron in the rocks. Later, the planet lost most of its water and its thick atmosphere, because Mars is small and has weak gravity.
The red dust is also lifted by wind. Mars has dust storms that sometimes cover the whole planet for weeks. That is why, even though some parts of the surface are actually dark grey or brown, the planet appears red overall. For next class, please read the chapter on the Martian moons.$q$, NULL, 1)
  RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What is the main topic of the lecture?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The professor explains why the planet looks red from Earth.$q$, 0) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$According to the professor, what causes the red colour?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The iron in the soil has formed iron oxide, i.e. rust.$q$, 1) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Why does Mars have little water and atmosphere today?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Mars is small and has weak gravity.'$q$, 2) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What is the effect of the dust storms?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$The dust makes the planet appear red even though some surfaces are grey or brown.$q$, 3) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What does the professor ask the students to do next?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Please read the chapter on the Martian moons.'$q$, 4) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$listening2$q$, $q$Listening — Conversation$q$, $q$listening$q$, 10, $q$لا يتضمن هذا القسم ملفًا صوتيًا بعد — اقرأ نص التسجيل كما لو كنت تسمعه (مرة واحدة فقط في الامتحان الحقيقي)، ثم أجب عن الأسئلة.$q$, $q$Conversation — a student and a professor

Student: Professor Hall, do you have a minute? I wanted to ask about the research paper that's due on Friday.
Professor: Of course, Maria. What's the problem?
Student: I've been working on it all week, but I got sick on Tuesday and lost two days. I'm worried I can't finish a good draft in time.
Professor: I'm sorry to hear that. Are you feeling better now?
Student: Yes, much better, thanks. Would it be possible to hand it in on Monday instead?
Professor: Normally I don't give extensions, but since you were ill, I can allow it. Do you have a doctor's note?
Student: I do. I'll bring it tomorrow.
Professor: Good. One thing, though. Monday is the absolute limit, because I need to return all the papers before the exam period.
Student: That's no problem. Thank you so much. Also, could I ask you about my sources? I'm not sure whether a website from a government agency counts as a reliable source.
Professor: Generally yes, if it publishes data and names the authors. Come to my office hours on Thursday and we can go through your list.$q$, NULL, 2)
  RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Why does the student visit the professor?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$She asks to hand in the paper on Monday instead of Friday.$q$, 0) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$Why does she need more time?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$She got sick on Tuesday and lost two days.$q$, 1) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What does the professor ask her to bring?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Do you have a doctor's note?'$q$, 2) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$What is the latest day she can submit the paper?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Monday is the absolute limit.'$q$, 3) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, v_sec, $q$When can she discuss her sources with the professor?$q$, 'multiple_choice', 1, $q$'Come to my office hours on Thursday.'$q$, 4) RETURNING id INTO v_q;
  VALUES (v_test, $q$Writing — Academic Discussion (ungraded practice)$q$, 10, $q$اكتب ردًا من 100 كلمة على الأقل تعبّر فيه عن رأيك وتضيف فكرة جديدة للنقاش. قارن مع النموذج بعد الانتهاء.$q$, $q$WRITING FOR AN ACADEMIC DISCUSSION (10 minutes)

Your professor is teaching a class on education. Write a post responding to the professor's question. In your response, you should express and support your opinion and contribute to the discussion in your own words. An effective response will contain at least 100 words.

Professor Lewis: This week we are discussing technology in the classroom. Some people say that students learn better when they use laptops and tablets in class, while others think these devices are distracting and that students should take notes by hand. Which view do you agree with, and why?

Student 1 (Ahmed): I think laptops are a great tool. I can search for information instantly, and my typing is much faster than my handwriting, so I don't miss anything the teacher says.

Student 2 (Julia): I disagree. Whenever I use my laptop, I end up checking messages or social media. When I write by hand, I stay focused and I remember the material better.$q$, $q$I agree more with Julia, although Ahmed makes a fair point about speed. Typing lets students record more of what a teacher says, but that is exactly the problem: if you try to write every word, you are not really thinking about the meaning. When I take notes by hand, I have to choose the key ideas and put them in my own words, and that process helps me understand and remember them.

Laptops are also a constant source of distraction. Even a student who intends to use one only for notes may be tempted by a notification, and research suggests that this also distracts the classmates sitting nearby.

That said, I do not think devices should be banned completely. They are useful for looking up a definition or for students with learning difficulties. A sensible compromise would be to use laptops only for specific activities and to take notes by hand during lectures.$q$);
END $ep$;

